<!--
  The typed coordination engine, as a `dotnet tool` (ADR-0034 Phase 3a).

  THE FLIP HAS SINCE HAPPENED (ADR-0040 Phase D COMPLETE). This tool is no longer packaged to *shadow*
  bash — it IS the coordination client. `scripts/fsgg-coord` is now the §4.4 shim that execs this
  command, the ~7,000-line bash monolith is deleted, and the shadow, the `engine=` mode flag, and the
  fleet-divergence ledger are retired. The history below is why it was packaged BEFORE the flip; it is
  kept as the record of that ordering.

  WHY IT WAS PACKAGED FIRST, AND WHY THAT WAS *NOT* THE FLIP.

  Phase 2 shipped the shadow: both engines decide, bash's answer is returned, the disagreement is
  logged, and bash stays authoritative until that log is clean. Phase 2's exit criterion is "zero
  divergence across the LIVE FLEET for three consecutive days" — and it could never be met, because
  the engine existed only where somebody had BUILT it, which is `.github` and nowhere else. Fleet
  evidence needs the engine distributed; distribution was scheduled for Phase 3; Phase 3 was gated on
  Phase 2 exiting. The clock could never start (.github#617).

  So publishing came BEFORE flipping, and it was the only order in which the flip's evidence could
  exist. Bash stayed authoritative through Phase 3a. The single thing packaging changed was that a
  worker in a receiver repo now HAD an engine to shadow with.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003; "package identity == product identity, never rename in place"):

    PackageId        FS.GG.Coord.Cli     — the name ADR-0034 §4.1 gives it, and the right one for what
                                           it BECOMES: at the flip this is the coordination client.
    ToolCommandName  fsgg-coord-engine   — and it never changes either. At the flip, the `kit:` row
                                           `scripts/fsgg-coord` becomes a shim that EXECS this command,
                                           so the command name never has to be renamed and no tool
                                           manifest ever has to be rewritten.

  STABLE, NOT -preview. The org promoted its last `-preview` producer in July, and the shared Renovate
  preset now pins to stable (`ignoreUnstable: true`). A `-preview` tool would be INVISIBLE to Renovate
  — and Renovate-invisibility is one of the stated reasons ADR-0034 §4.4 rejected the self-contained
  binary. Shipping a tool the update fabric cannot see would defeat the point of shipping it.

  `.github` builds this FROM SOURCE and never restores it from the feed. That asymmetry is what breaks
  the publish cycle: a broken feed cannot stop the coordination tool being fixed, because the repo that
  fixes it does not depend on the feed. Only RECEIVERS consume the package.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsgg-coord-engine</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.13.0 to 0.14.0 is a MINOR, and under 0.x SemVer that is also the BREAKING signal — the rule
         registry/dependencies.yml's coord-engine row states twice: "on the 0.x line an observable-to-
         callers behaviour change is spelled a minor, because 0.x has no major to take" (at 0.13.0), and
         "a 0.x minor is the only spelling available for it" (at 0.12.0). Patch is not defensible here;
         THREE of the six shipped commits force the minor independently (.github#1761):

           * #1646 (b047b1a) — `claim` and `take` now REFUSE a `worker` flag naming somebody else over
             another worker's live lock. A call 0.13.0 PERMITS now fails. The 0.12.0 case verbatim.
           * #1628 (0beb561) — the blocking count ranks over the WHOLE board rather than the
             repo-scoped candidate set, so `batch` and `next` can select a DIFFERENT item for the same
             board. Scheduling order is what every caller of those verbs consumes.
           * #1541 (cb5e291) — `reconcile` with the `apply` and `json` flags together is now ACCEPTED
             by the parser and emits a machine projection; 0.13.0 rejects the combination.

         #1636 (debbce6) additionally changes a computed KitDigest for the same input tree.

         This property is the RELEASE ACT (see scripts/check-engine-freshness.py): the engine's version
         moves only at release time, by hand, and release-coord-engine.yml evaluates it with
         `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Version` and refuses any `coord-engine/v[version]` tag that
         disagrees. The tag is a coherence check against this line and never its source. -->
    <Version>0.14.0</Version>
    <AssemblyName>fsgg-coord-engine</AssemblyName>

    <Description>FS-GG typed coordination engine — the schedulability model behind `fsgg-coord` as one total function over a board-state snapshot, plus the typed IO layer that drives it. It IS the coordination tool (ADR-0034/ADR-0040): the `fsgg-coord` shim resolves and execs it; there is no bash left to shadow.</Description>
    <Authors>FS-GG</Authors>
    <Company>FS-GG</Company>
    <PackageTags>fsgg;coordination;scheduler;dotnet-tool;fsharp</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github/tree/main/src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageProjectUrl>

    <!-- THE RELEASE NOTES ARE WHERE THE BREAK IS ANNOUNCED, and that is the point of them here.
         nuget.org renders this on the package listing and `dotnet tool` consumers read it there. A
         changelog that said only "fixes" while a previously-accepted flag started exiting 1 would be
         the #266 shape in its purest form: the org knowing a thing, and the consumer discovering it
         from a broken script. Criterion 5 of .github#1596 asks for exactly this.

         KEEP THIS PROPERTY IN STEP WITH <Version> ABOVE. The 0.13.0 cut moved <Version> and left these
         notes reading "0.12.0 — BREAKING...", so the nuget.org listing for 0.13.0 announced the PREVIOUS
         release's break and none of its own seven commits. Nothing gates the two against each other
         (.github#1762). A consumer who reads the listing and finds the wrong release described has been
         failed by it exactly as much as one who finds nothing. -->
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.14.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line, which is also the 0.x BREAKING signal.

Six engine commits since 0.13.0. None touches the wire surface (src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Protocol.fs), so
the exit codes and verb contract this repo's skills document are the ones this build enforces.

THE REASON TO TAKE THIS RELEASE: three of the six are fixes to the CLAIM PROTOCOL, found in production
on 2026-07-27 when seven workers ran concurrently against it. Every worker vendors `scripts/fsgg-coord`,
which resolves and execs this package, so a receiver on 0.13.0 is running the protocol without them.

BREAKING: a session is not a credential (.github#1646). `verifyHeld` granted `Held` when the claim
marker's id matched the id `--worker` NAMED and the two sessions were not known-different. Subagents
share a session id, so an impersonator satisfied both legs and could act on a lock they did not hold.
The refusal is enforced in the ENGINE at both of `Held`'s doors — `verifyHeld` and `claim` — so every
verb that takes the capability is covered by construction: `release`, `heartbeat`, `widen`, `done`'s
lock-drop, and the ADR-0041 chore lock. A call 0.13.0 accepted now exits non-zero.

  $ fsgg-coord-engine claim .github#123 --worker someone-else   # 0.13.0: may succeed
                                                                # 0.14.0: refused

WHO THIS BREAKS: anything that passed a `--worker` it does not own — which was never a supported act,
but was reachable. The remedy is to pass your OWN minted id (`whoami --mint`), or `--force` if you
really mean to steal, which is loud and posts the theft.

ALSO in the same fix: `claim --force --worker THEM` used to destroy a third party's lock and sign the
theft notice with the INNOCENT worker's id, falsifying the accounting rather than merely bypassing it.
The notice now names the actor.

BREAKING for consumers of scheduling ORDER: the blocking count ranks over the WHOLE board, not the
--repo-scoped candidate set (.github#1628). `batch` and `next` can therefore choose a different item
for the same board than 0.13.0 would. The ranking is more correct — a row blocking ten items across
three repos outranks one blocking two in this repo — but it is an observable change for any caller that
depended on the previous pick.

NEW PROJECTION: `reconcile --apply --json` now PARSES (.github#1541). 0.13.0 rejected the combination,
so the org's one mutating reconcile verb had no machine projection at all and callers scraped prose.

FIXES:

  * The chore offer's board read is FRESH (.github#1679). It read the board through the 90-second
    scheduling cache, so a CLASS-PROJECTION-LAG chore survived the very write that discharged it and
    was offered again to the next worker. A signal that lies, in a fabric where workers are told to
    trust signals. Fixed with a third ReadIntent rather than by borrowing Reconciling's warrant.
  * BLOCKER-CLEARED respects the human-park sentinel it would overwrite (.github#1644). A row carrying
    `Blocked on: human/...` was promoted to Ready by the very chore that is supposed to honour it.
  * KitDigest hashes TWO runtime skill roots, not three (.github#1636) — .codex/skills is retired
    (ADR-0065 as amended). This changes the computed digest for the same input tree.</PackageReleaseNotes>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- RestorePackagesWithLockFile, RestoreLockedMode and DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder (ADR-0006,
         ADR-0032) are INHERITED from the root Directory.Build.props, which imports the org-shared
         config this repo authors and ships. Every project here used to set them by hand because
         .github did not consume its own build config (#609). It does now. -->

    <!-- The engine is invoked once per scheduling call, on every worker, in a fan-out. Startup cost
         is the whole of its runtime cost, so it takes no dependency it does not need. No
         Spectre.Console here: JSON is the contract, and the text projection is a handful of printfn. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Json.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Json.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fs" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Client.fs" />
    <!-- AFTER `Client.fs`, deliberately: the follow-up queue reuses `parseRefIn` and the exit constants
         rather than growing a second answer to "what is a ref?" or "what does an empty look exit?" (#485).
         It is a LOCAL verb — no token, no board — and depends on `Client` for those two things only. -->
    <Compile Include="Followups.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Followups.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.Core/FS.GG.Coord.Core.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
